A||N||4||bHooper||8?'s for the paint experts||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||08-18-2000||11:53 AM||||my car is in severe need of a paint job, and i wanted to ask about cost, prepwork, ect.<P>the car is a red '86 GT, that was seen its better days.  the paint is cracked and peeling off of the bumper and is "dead" on the spoiler.  there are also spider cracks in on the rear bumper.  also, the clearcoat is peeling on the hood and rough (like overspray) on the roof.<P>so... question time.<BR>1) how should i approach having the car repainted?  (i don't want to (can't) spent $2000 on the car)<BR>2) would it be cheaper for me to strip the bumpers myself?  if so, what is the best way to go about this?<BR>3) will the spider cracks cause any problems in the repaint (even though the old paint will be removed)?<BR>4) if i strip the bumpers, what kind of product should i use?  also, should i primer them myself?  if so, what product should i use for this?<BR>5) do body shops prefer to paint with all the panels on the car, or is it better to have them removed?<BR>6) if i want to have the car painted all one color, or a different color, does all the old paint need to be removed?  i am considering a darker red (not burgundy), a poppy green (like mg/lotus/triumphs), and a titainum silver.<BR>7) with the various prices of paint jobs, what should i expect to pay for something comperable to "factory", or better?<BR>8) did i forget to ask anything?<P>thanks in advance for your comments/advice.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phoenix||08-18-2000||12:18 PM||||I just finished repainting by coupe. Here's what i did:<BR>First I didn't use a chemical stripper (too messy and could screw-up the plastic panels).<BR>I sanded all of the clear coat and most of the color off with 220 grit using a DA air sander and an electric palm sander. On the the areas with spider cracks I took off all of the paint. Spayed on one coat of DP-50LF primer and followed with a coat of sanding primer. sanded out with 320. Sprayed another coat of primer and sanded out with 400. Sprayed a sealer coat of DP-50LF reduced 25%.<BR>Sprayed 3 coats of Singe Step DCC Concept 2000 color.<BR>My total material costs for paint was about $320.00 (Red is one the most expensive colors). Shops get a better price on paint so with their labor and materials you could expect at least $400 to $500 for a decent job and more for better work.  To do something better than original it's labor intensive and you get what you pay for  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-18-2000||01:23 PM||||I'd not use a chem strip on these cars. no need to strip them anyway. simply sanding out all the damaged areas then scuffing the whole car followed with chem prep. the prep chemical removes any residual oil wax etc. It's sold under a number of names.<P>Using a flex agent in the paint isn't a bad idea. depends exactly what paint and application process you are using wether you need it or not. (It's all sprayed but there are many methods/techniques of spraying.)<P>The main thing is proper sanding..... You can do it yourself the more you do the less the painter needs to do and the less you'll pay. <P>the panels are close to the same hardness as the piaint. might actually be softer. you can dig holes in them really quick. best thing to use is a random orbit sander and keep it moving. Don't use sand papers any more coarse than about 220. Use 220 to take off the flakes and start feathering, then move to 400 and 600 to finish. HAND scuff the whole car with WET 600-1200 to break the old surface. Leave the chem prep for the shop or have them do it a second time.  You want the prep to remove all road grime you pickup on the way there.Doing it twice won't hurt and makes sure everything is removed.<P>Color changes cost more because the painter has to cover allot of detail areas. edges of doors, fender flanges, that sort of thing that normally isn't shot in a basic repaint to OE color. Anything that complicates the job raises price.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||08-21-2000||09:53 AM||||Ok, let me see if I a understand this at all.<P>If I am going to prep the car, it is better to use a orbital type sander, and definately not use any chemical stripers.<P>What about the mirrors and drip rails?  Should I remove these?... I am considering having the mirrors done body color, as well as the ground effects.<P>As for primer... should I let the paint shop do this?  Or does the stuff from the can work as good?<P>I saw a Maco ad, for $175 paint job.  Now, I now this is a <B>cheap</B> paint job... but just how bad is this?  If I prep the car, except for a little body work, could I expect something at least factory?  If I did that, I would be willing to pay extra for more clearcoat... but do they sand the paint, b4 aplying clearcoat?<P>JK... Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||80's BOY||08-21-2000||01:31 PM||||you've got some great feedback so far, but i must give info about my experience. alot of these two-bit paint jobs for 100 bucks or so are a rip-off. if you do your own body work before hand, you MAY come out ahead. otherwise they just pull the car in and spray it. no buffing, finish work or detailing at all. talk to a good body shop and ask him everything you can do on your own before the car comes in for work. let him mix some primer and run a tab on it if you want to spray it yourself. that way you know all the elemants will match up. some over the counter primers won't mix with high quality paint chemicals and vice versa.do it panel by panel and take off the ground effects and trim. it will prevent peeling in the long run. good luck!!||||reg||10
